Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.909 align:start position:0%
 
hello i think it's going  steve kaufman here again the linguist on  language it's uh boxing day in canada  the 26th of december  uh i've spent some time up snowshoeing  on cyprus mountain it was a glorious day  it was sunny the snow couldn't have been  whiter i was up there with my wife and  with my  son and grandchildren and we had a great  time  what i want to talk about here  is bilingual texts  why do i want to talk about it because  it was raised by ilya who is one of the  regular  contributors to our  blog at the linguist on language always  has penetrating questions about language  learning and he's also someone who is  designing his own  software for language learning  and it's also something that has been  discussed on this  site that i sometimes go to called how  to learn any language language.com  and  the subject of the discussion  certainly on how to learn any language  was that  one way to learn a language is to get a  hold of a bilingual text book  and that very early on in your language  learning and according to some even at  the very beginning you can start reading  works of literature by reading the  language you are learning which is  sometimes called l2  on the left hand side  and l1 which is your own language or a  language you're very familiar with  on the right-hand side and so you read  ahead in your familiar language and then  you struggle through with the other  language and it was also suggested that  you could be listening to  l2 the target language while reading l1  which is the familiar language so that  you start to hear these words and the  sounds of the language while you are  following along the meaning in in a  language that you are very comfortable  reading  so  and  i guess i'm going to talk about what i  think of this approach and also try to  answer ilya who has raised the very  interesting point that  very often  automatic or computer translation  is more helpful at least to him  than a more literary translation  of something and he cites the example  where at link we have the power of the  linguist which is a very simple story  for beginners we have it in four or five  languages and he finds the computer  generated translation more helpful to  his language learning and he's a  beginner in spanish in this case  more helpful than um the actual man-made  translation so where do i what do i  think of all of this  let's begin by saying that reading in my  opinion is extremely important to  language learning now yes there are  people in the bazaar in morocco or you  know all kinds of people who become  quite fluent in another language and  never learn to read and there are many  people who when faced with languages  like japanese or chinese with very  difficult writing systems simply learn  to speak and they do it quite well so  yeah you can learn without reading but  if you learn to read you will learn  faster you will learn more words you  will have an easier time  um and reading is is um you know i i've  had a i bought a book over over  christmas called um  the called proust and the squid and it's  written by uh marianne wolfe and it  talks about reading and how we develop  the ability to read and how this  represents something very very new for  our brains  and she points out how proust  says famous french writer that when we  read we have that tremendous opportunity  to communicate with people from another  time and place even sitting in the  solitude of our own room  and so reading does wonderful things  and it is certainly very very  useful for language learning  and i would add reading and listening  because now we have this tremendous  capability with  mp3 files and the manipulation of sound  files that we can we can listen to  anything we want wherever we want  so reading and listening i have said it  many times and and when i saw stephen  krashem uh explain this whole concept of  meaningful input i said wow that's  exactly the way i feel about language  learning so he is both an inspiration to  me and also someone who has sort of  confirmed what i have learned  so much on my own and i've got to check  the time here how much time have i used  up okay  uh so  i believe that reading bilingual books  is distracting  it's something i do occasionally if i do  not certainly before i had link before i  had the opportunity to look up words  uh  on the internet save the words to a  database do all the things that we do in  link  yes i in my russian at a certain point  did take along some bilingual books if i  was traveling somewhere  i didn't have access to the computer i  would read along a little bit in english  a little bit in in russian  i don't like doing it because it's  distracting because you tend to leave  the russian in my case if i'm learning  russian you go off to the english  you forget a little bit of what you saw  there you come back you're still  struggling with the russian there are  still words that you don't know what  they mean  as you get better you then go  and look at the english even when you  don't need to  you'd be better off just to look up the  few words that you need  so my preference is for reading  difficult text on the computer with  access to an online dictionary and  preferably in an environment like link  where you can capture the words and then  review them later on reviewing words  that you have  saved from a text  that you have identified as words that  you need to learn reviewing these words  in a variety of ways is very helpful and  useful  even though  you will probably pick up most of your  words through your extensive reading  but the review of the words even if it's  not thorough even if it isn't every word  that you review it forces you to look at  the more closely it is in my view very  useful in the whole process of acquiring  vocabulary so i'm not a fan of bilingual  texts i tried it in different ways i  have also tried listening to  russian while reading english reading  anna karenina for example  one thing i will agree with ilya if i'm  reading in russian and i have trouble  with a paragraph  i prefer to take that paragraph and put  it onto a computer translation site and  get the translation and pick through and  so now i've got a sense of that  paragraph i go back and i save the words  that i need but i don't want to  systematically be reading every  paragraph  in  the native language or a familiar  language and i don't want to be tempted  to go there all the time and that's the  trouble with notes as well all the  explanatory notes or words  that they identify as the words that you  need to look up all of this to me is  distracting i prefer to fight my way  through a text words that i don't know i  click on them i look them up and they go  on my list  so  i was surprised that ilya found the um  the computer-generated translation more  helpful for the power of the linguist  which is a simple text  i found when i tried the power of the  linguist in  italian and japanese even though i speak  both languages when i put them onto the  computer generated translation i got  junk bag not at all helpful i have found  that computer generated translation  works very well for russian that may  have something to do with the people who  who obviously do the software so  where do i stand on this bilingual text  it's a matter of taste some people like  them and generated a lot of interest at  how to learn any language i'm not a fan  i've tried it i find it distracting i  prefer to get into  the language that i'm learning stay in  that environment try to read it listen  to it  and i only leave that language when i  know that i need help so i decided i  need to learn look up that word i  decided that i have trouble with this  paragraph and i go outside and get help  i should add that  even if you don't 100  understand the paragraph it really  doesn't matter look up the words you  looked up some words you still don't  understand it i just plow on  and eventually two months later i'll  read that text again and i will  understand it  so  uh there are lots of bilingual texts  available it's a little more difficult  to find them  uh that's another thing it's it's much  easier to find uh text which are not  bilingual  and there is so much help now on the  internet that i personally think that's  the way to go so ilya that's an answer  to you and uh to your question and i  look forward to hearing uh you know  people's opinion on this  and i'm also gonna answer another of  elia's questions which has to do with  uh how to use this sort of recording of  word lists from link but i'll deal that  with that in a separate podcast and  webcast thank you very much for  listening come and visit us at  lingq.com link  or the linguistic language of course  which is the linguist.blogs.com  thank you for listening

